JNEXT @VERSION@ for web pages
=============================

JNEXT is a ZX Spectrum Next emulator. This is its browser build: a page loads
jnext-@VERSION@.js with a <script> tag and starts a player with jnext.mount().
Keep jnext-@VERSION@.wasm in the same directory as the .js file.

plain.html, itch-game.html, itch-host.html and widget.html are example pages,
explained in the user guide's chapter "JNEXT in a web page". widget.html uses
jnext-widget.js, a ready-made player: a div, one script tag. Serve them from a
web server over https (or http://localhost): a page opened as a local file
cannot load the .wasm, and the player needs a secure context.

jnext-debug-bridge.mjs is a node script (node 18 or later, no other dependency) that lets a debugger
which speaks TCP (DeZog, z88dk-gdb) reach a page whose player has the `debug` option: run
`node jnext-debug-bridge.mjs --ws-port 12000 --tcp-port 11000`. It is explained in the user guide's chapter
"JNEXT in a web page", section "Debugging from the page". It has no authentication: keep it on the
loopback address.

This package contains no part of NextZXOS and no ROM. NextZXOS is SpecNext's
copyrighted software, and JNEXT does not ship, host or serve it. The player
gets the official distribution from specnext.com, or asks the visitor to
download it from there and choose it, and makes its SD card in the visitor's
browser, which keeps it for the site. A page needs to supply nothing for this;
the example pages take the program from their query string (?file=PROGRAM) or
from the constants at the top of plain.html.

License: GNU General Public License, version 3 (LICENSE). Authors and
